A `Depends(...)` marker is only resolved where it appears as a parameter of a callable that FastAPI itself solves. In your code it is passed as an ordinary constructor argument at import time, so `self.greeting_provider` holds a plain `Depends` object that FastAPI never sees, and calling it raises the TypeError.

What FastAPI checks is that the dependency is a callable (function, class, or anything else) and the parameters defined; it analyzes those parameters and processes them the same way as a path operation function's parameters, including sub-dependencies. So declare the sub-dependency as a parameter of the callable you hand to `Depends`:

```python
class GreetingGenerator:
    def __init__(self, greeting: Annotated[str, Depends(get_greeting_message)]):
        self.greeting = greeting

@app.get("/hello")
async def hello(g: Annotated[GreetingGenerator, Depends(GreetingGenerator)]):
    return {"message": g.greeting}
```

This is the same shape as `CommonQueryParams` and as `query_or_cookie_extractor`, which takes `q: Annotated[str, Depends(query_extractor)]`. An already-built instance can also be the dependency, as with `Depends(oauth2_scheme)`.

The sections do not cover obtaining a resolved dependency as a callable you invoke yourself later.

