`Depends(...)` is not a value you can hold onto or call yourself. It is a marker that FastAPI resolves only when it appears in the parameters of a callable that FastAPI itself calls: from `Depends(x)` FastAPI extracts x's declared parameters and x is what it actually calls. Passing `Depends(get_greeting_message)` into `GreetingGenerator.__init__` at module level just stores a `Depends` object on the instance, so `self.greeting_provider()` raises `TypeError: 'Depends' object is not callable`.

Declare the sub-dependency in the signature of the callable FastAPI solves instead. What FastAPI checks is that the dependency is a callable (function, class, or anything else) and the parameters defined; it analyzes those parameters and processes them the same way as path operation function parameters, including sub-dependencies:

```python
class GreetingGenerator:
    def __call__(self, message: Annotated[str, Depends(get_greeting_message)]):
        return {"message": message}

greeter = GreetingGenerator()
```

Keep `Annotated[dict, Depends(greeter)]` on the path operation. FastAPI then solves `get_greeting_message` and its own `get_user_browser` sub-dependency per request.

The sections do not describe deferring resolution so you can invoke a dependency callable yourself later.

