Your route is never registered. You wrote app.get("/") as a plain function call. It has to be applied as a decorator, with the @ sign:

    @app.get("/")
    async def root():
        return {"message": "Hello World"}

As the docs put it, @app.get("/") tells FastAPI that the function right below is in charge of handling requests that go to the path / using a get operation. Without the @, the call just runs at import time and the function underneath is never attached to any path, so the app has no path operations at all. That matches both symptoms exactly: {"detail":"Not Found"} on / and an empty /docs page.

Add the @ and both the endpoint and its docs entry will appear. This is unrelated to CORS, to the browser you use, and to your Python version, so the middleware you added is not what was fixing or blocking anything.

